+### C24. `ftrl`'s decay shrinks the coefficients toward zero instead of forgetting rows
+
+`crates/online-core/src/ftrl.rs`, `step` (the `lam != 1.0` block and
+`weight_of`) and `predict`. The module doc: "the accumulators decayed
+by the same clock as every other model here so it forgets on the same
+schedule." What the decay does is something else, and it is the one
+thing T-R1 cannot see, because T-R1 runs at `halflife = inf`.
+
+FTRL-Proximal's closed form `b = −(z − sgn(z)·l1) / ((β + √n)/α + l2)`
+minimises `Σ_s g_s·b + ½ Σ_s σ_s‖b − b_s‖² + l1‖b‖₁ + ½ l2‖b‖²`, and it
+is closed-form only because the anchor weights telescope: `Σ_s σ_s = (β
++ √n_t)/α`. `z` carries `Σ g_s − Σ σ_s b_s`, whose second term is `≈
+−b*·√n/α` once the fit is near `b*`. Multiplying `z` by `lam` scales
+that anchor term by `lam`; multiplying `n` by `lam` scales the
+denominator's `√n` by `√lam`. Their ratio is the coefficient, and
+`lam/√lam = √lam`. So:
+
+- On a row the model does not learn from -- a null target, a zero
+  weight, a `strict_binary` refusal, a C2 overflow skip, and every row
+  `predict` scores at clock distance `d` from the last learned one --
+  the coefficients shrink by `√lam^d` (by `lam^d` early on, while `β/α
+  + l2` dominates `√n/α`). A hundred null-target rows at halflife 200
+  take a slope of `1.47` to `1.08`; at halflife 50, `1.37` to `0.42`
+  (at `squared_loss_fits_a_continuous_target`'s settings, `α = 0.5`,
+  `l2 = 0.01`). Every other coefficient model here leaves its
+  coefficients where they were on such a row: `ewridge` and `lasso`
+  are mean-form, so numerator and denominator decay together; `rls`,
+  `kalman` and `sgd` do not touch `beta`; and `pa`'s own
+  `null_target_is_predict_only` asserts exactly this. Under `predict`
+  the same state scores the same features differently according to how
+  far the row is from the last learned row.
+- Under learning the gradient has to keep replacing what the decay
+  removes, and the fixed point is `b = b*·E[z²] / (E[z²] + (1 − lam)·D
+  − s̄)`, with `D = (β + √n_∞)/α + l2` and `s̄` the mean per-row `σ`. At
+  the defaults (`α = 0.1`, `β = 1`, `l2 = 1`) on a unit-variance feature
+  with `y = 1.5·x`: halflife 200 gives `1.33` noise-free and `0.97` at
+  unit noise; halflife 50 gives `0.83` and `0.63`; `inf` gives `1.50`
+  and `1.49`. An EW-OLS at the same halflife -- what `ewridge`
+  converges to -- gives `1.50`, `1.41`, `1.50`, `1.33`, `1.50`, `1.49`
+  respectively, and its distance from `1.5` is sampling error at that
+  halflife, not bias. So `ftrl` at any finite `halflife`, which is the
+  library's ordinary use, is a biased estimator of the relationship
+  every other model in the bank estimates without bias, by an amount
+  that grows with `1 − lam`, with the noise, and with `1/α`. Logistic is
+  the same on the logit scale: a base rate of `0.9` on noise features
+  comes out at `p = 0.76` at halflife 200 (intercept `1.16` for `logit
+  0.9 = 2.20`) and `0.90` at `inf`. The in-crate tests all run at
+  `halflife = inf` except `forgets_on_the_clock`, which asks only that
+  a flipped rule is followed -- and a shrinking estimator follows it.
+
+The sizes above are from a forty-line Python transcription of `step`
+run in pass 10, not from the crate; the mechanism and the direction are
+from the code.
+
+The fix is the sum the closed form stands in for. Keep the decayed
+anchor weight explicitly -- `sig_i ← lam·sig_i + σ_i` per coordinate,
+beside `zz` and `n` -- and read the coefficient as `b = −(z −
+sgn(z)·l1) / (sig + l2)`. On a row that learns nothing `z` and `sig`
+decay together and `b` is invariant, as it is everywhere else here;
+under learning it is the FTRL-Proximal solution to the exponentially
+forgotten objective `Σ_s lam^{age_s}·[g_s·b + ½σ_s‖b − b_s‖²] + l1‖b‖₁ +
+½l2‖b‖²`, which is what "forgets on the same schedule" ought to mean;
+and at `halflife = inf`, `sig = (β + √n)/α` exactly, so T-R1 is
+untouched. `n` goes on decaying for the learning rate, which is right
+-- an EW-RMS of the gradient is the point of `n`. One more
+`Vec<Vec<f64>>` in the state, so a schema bump; `predict` applies `lam`
+to all three. (Decaying `n` alone is not an alternative: `z` then grows
+without bound against a bounded denominator.)
+
